NAME
       sealert - explain SELinux denials in plain words

SYNOPSIS
       sealert -a FILE
       sealert -l ID

DESCRIPTION
       sealert belongs to the setroubleshoot-server package (not installed
       at first: dnf install setroubleshoot-server). It reads the AVC denials
       that SELinux writes to /var/log/audit/audit.log and, for each one,
       says what was blocked and suggests fixes with a confidence score:
       usually a restorecon, a semanage port or fcontext rule, or a boolean.

       When the package is installed, the setroubleshoot service also logs
       a one-line summary of each new denial to the journal, ending with a
       "sealert -l ID" command that shows the full report.

OPTIONS
       -a FILE
              Analyse every denial in FILE (usually the audit log).

       -l ID  Show the report for one alert, by the ID from the journal.

EXAMPLES
       Explain the denials logged so far:

           sealert -a /var/log/audit/audit.log

       Find the alert IDs in the journal:

           journalctl -t setroubleshoot

NOTES
       Read the suggestions critically: the first one (often restorecon or
       semanage) is usually right; making a custom module with audit2allow
       is rarely the answer.

SEE ALSO
       ausearch(8), audit2why(1), restorecon(8), semanage(8), setsebool(8)
